# runtime-denial-write.layer-a
# Tenant submission: a program whose harm cannot be decided structurally.
#
# Intent: an agent submitted a writer that declares Filesystem.Write and writes
# to the path "/tenant/secret.log". The declared signature is honest -- the
# authored EffectDecl pins the refinement to the same literal the call passes, so
# Q-039 projection agreement holds and the program PASSES verification. Whether
# this is harmful depends on a value (the path) and on the grant the host hands
# it -- a runtime question, not a structural one.
#
# Used by S3 (runtime denial): the host grants Filesystem.Write refined to a
# DIFFERENT path ("/tenant/out.log"). At the call site the runtime evaluates the
# requested path against the held grant; no granted pattern covers
# "/tenant/secret.log", so the call is denied with a RefinementViolation and a
# structured DenialReport (category, requested vs held, node, phase). The tenant
# terminates; a co-tenant submitted in the same batch completes normally.
@v=1 root=writeApp
strT PRM String
bytesT PRM Bytes
writeFx EFC "Filesystem.Write" [strT]
writeT FNT [strT bytesT] intT
writeFn FN "strand-builtin:Fs.Write" writeT [writeFx] "writeFx:0"
targetPath STR "/tenant/secret.log"
payload BYT "deadbeef"
writeDecl EFD writeFx [targetPath]
writeApp APP writeFn [targetPath payload] [] [writeDecl]
